1
00:00:44,596 --> 00:00:49,058
INDIANA JONES AND THE KINGDOM
OF THE CRYSTAL SKULL

2
00:01:21,174 --> 00:01:22,800
What have you got in there?

3
00:01:22,884 --> 00:01:24,636
Want to race?

4
00:01:24,719 --> 00:01:26,763
Come on!

5
00:01:38,733 --> 00:01:41,944
- Please?
- Come on!

6
00:02:02,590 --> 00:02:04,592
Faster!

7
00:02:52,974 --> 00:02:54,100
Excuse me.

8
00:02:54,183 --> 00:02:57,061
This area is closed
for weapons testing.

9
00:02:57,145 --> 00:03:00,106
That includes all personnel.

10
00:03:00,189 --> 00:03:01,983
Good afternoon, sir.

11
00:03:09,157 --> 00:03:10,783
I'm afraid that includes you, too.

12
00:03:10,867 --> 00:03:12,201
The Pentagon.

13
00:03:28,342 --> 00:03:29,635
RESTRICTED AREA

14
00:04:36,369 --> 00:04:37,703
Russians.

15
00:04:39,497 --> 00:04:41,833
This isn't going to be easy.

16
00:04:41,916 --> 00:04:43,668
Not as easy as it used to be.

17
00:04:43,751 --> 00:04:46,546
- We've faced worse.
- When?

18
00:04:46,629 --> 00:04:48,548
In Flensburg. There were twice as many.

19
00:04:48,631 --> 00:04:50,967
- We were younger.
- I'm still young!

20
00:04:51,050 --> 00:04:52,301
We had guns.

21
00:04:52,385 --> 00:04:56,180
Put your hands down.
You're embarrassing us.

22
00:04:56,264 --> 00:04:58,891
I bet $500 we get out of this.

23
00:05:08,943 --> 00:05:10,611
Let's say $100.

24
00:05:14,365 --> 00:05:16,534
You recognize the building, yes?

25
00:05:30,214 --> 00:05:32,174
Excuse me.

26
00:05:32,258 --> 00:05:34,635
You should have said:
"Drop dead, comrade."

27
00:05:52,153 --> 00:05:55,072
- Where did you find it?
- In Mexico.

28
00:05:55,156 --> 00:05:58,701
They were digging,
looking for these things.

29
00:06:03,748 --> 00:06:06,167
You're not from around here, are you?

30
00:06:06,250 --> 00:06:10,838
Where do you imagine I'm from,
Dr. Jones?

31
00:06:11,839 --> 00:06:14,008
From your accent,

32
00:06:14,091 --> 00:06:17,511
I'd say eastern Ukraine.

33
00:06:17,595 --> 00:06:19,555
Top of the class.

34
00:06:19,639 --> 00:06:21,724
I'm Colonel Dr. Irina Spalko.

35
00:06:23,601 --> 00:06:26,979
I've received the Order of Lenin
three times.

36
00:06:27,063 --> 00:06:31,108
Also the Hero
of Socialist Labor medal.

37
00:06:31,192 --> 00:06:32,526
And why?

38
00:06:33,778 --> 00:06:38,783
Because I know things.
I know them before anyone else.

39
00:06:38,866 --> 00:06:42,078
And what I don't know, I find out.

40
00:06:42,954 --> 00:06:47,875
What I need to know now
is inside here.

41
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:06,602
You have a difficult mind to read,
Dr. Jones.

42
00:07:06,686 --> 00:07:07,687
Ow.

43
00:07:07,770 --> 00:07:12,566
So we'll do this, how do you say,
the old-fashioned way.

44
00:07:12,650 --> 00:07:16,153
You'll tell us. You'll help us
find what we're looking for.

45
00:07:38,342 --> 00:07:40,803
This warehouse is where
you and your government...

46
00:07:40,886 --> 00:07:43,597
...have hidden your secrets.

47
00:07:43,681 --> 00:07:47,977
This is a military warehouse.
I've never been here before.

48
00:07:49,603 --> 00:07:51,814
Object sought: rectangular container,

49
00:07:51,897 --> 00:07:55,609
dimensions two meters
by half a meter by 66 centimeters.

50
00:07:55,693 --> 00:07:58,779
Contents of box: mummified remains.

51
00:07:58,863 --> 00:08:01,282
Doesn't that sound familiar?

52
00:08:01,365 --> 00:08:04,785
Why do you think I know what you mean?

53
00:08:04,869 --> 00:08:06,954
Because ten years ago...

54
00:08:07,038 --> 00:08:10,082
...you were part
of the team that examined it.

55
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:18,215
Even if I knew what you were talking about...

56
00:08:18,299 --> 00:08:21,802
You will help us find it.

57
00:08:33,064 --> 00:08:35,608
A compass. I need a compass.

58
00:08:35,691 --> 00:08:37,401
You know, north, south, east...

59
00:08:37,485 --> 00:08:38,819
West.

60
00:08:40,404 --> 00:08:42,073
Don't you have a compass?

61
00:08:43,991 --> 00:08:45,868
I need your bullets.

62
00:08:53,375 --> 00:08:56,837
The contents of that box
are magnetized.

63
00:08:56,921 --> 00:08:58,047
I need gunpowder.

64
00:08:58,130 --> 00:09:01,050
Do you want my help or not?

65
00:09:13,938 --> 00:09:18,109
Don't play games with me, Dr. Jones.
What are you trying to do?

66
00:09:18,192 --> 00:09:20,111
If it's still magnetized,

67
00:09:20,194 --> 00:09:23,405
the metal in this powder
will tell us where it is.

68
00:09:53,769 --> 00:09:56,689
Cartridges. Give me
shotgun shells.

69
00:09:59,984 --> 00:10:01,193
Pliers.

70
00:12:02,940 --> 00:12:04,149
ROSWELL, NEW MEXICO 1947

71
00:13:19,266 --> 00:13:23,103
Drop your guns!
Or I'll kill Colonel Dr. Spalko.

72
00:13:44,041 --> 00:13:45,751
Why, Mac?

73
00:13:45,834 --> 00:13:48,212
What do you want me to say, Jonesy?

74
00:13:48,295 --> 00:13:51,632
I'm a capitalist, and they pay.

75
00:13:53,467 --> 00:13:56,720
After all those years
spying on the Reds?

76
00:13:58,806 --> 00:14:01,183
I thought we were friends.

77
00:14:01,266 --> 00:14:04,395
I hit a bad streak
at the card table, mate.

78
00:14:04,478 --> 00:14:07,022
Terrible. The worst.

79
00:14:08,649 --> 00:14:11,318
I can't go <i>home</i>
empty-handed.

80
00:14:12,027 --> 00:14:15,197
Any last words of defiance,
Dr. Jones?

81
00:14:17,658 --> 00:14:19,868
Long live Eisenhower.

82
00:14:19,952 --> 00:14:21,453
Drop the gun.

83
00:14:22,287 --> 00:14:23,956
Done, mate.

84
00:14:50,858 --> 00:14:53,277
Gosh, I thought it was closer.

85
00:14:57,489 --> 00:14:58,740
Come on, come on!

86
00:15:17,176 --> 00:15:19,219
He mustn't get out alive!
Block the exits!

87
00:15:25,225 --> 00:15:27,728
Don't get smart, Boris.
You don't know him.

88
00:15:28,604 --> 00:15:30,731
You don't know him! You don't know him!

89
00:17:13,959 --> 00:17:15,460
Well done.

90
00:18:55,394 --> 00:18:57,979
Hello? Hello?

91
00:18:58,063 --> 00:19:03,735
<i>It's Howdy Doody time!</i>

92
00:19:03,819 --> 00:19:05,070
How?

93
00:19:06,446 --> 00:19:08,573
I knocked! Don't you have a pho...?

94
00:19:15,956 --> 00:19:17,374
Hold on.

95
00:19:17,457 --> 00:19:19,209
<i>Come on, kids!</i>

96
00:19:19,292 --> 00:19:22,212
<i>It's time to say goodbye!</i>

97
00:19:32,305 --> 00:19:34,307
That can't be good.

98
00:19:36,685 --> 00:19:40,063
<i>Attention, all personnel,
one minute to zero hour.</i>

99
00:19:40,313 --> 00:19:42,899
<i>Put on your goggles or turn away.</i>

100
00:19:42,983 --> 00:19:48,238
<i>Do not remove your goggles or look
until ten seconds afterward.</i>

101
00:19:48,321 --> 00:19:49,823
This can't be good at all.

102
00:19:57,539 --> 00:20:01,334
Wait! Wait, wait!

103
00:20:04,254 --> 00:20:06,590
Of course!
Don't wait for me!

104
00:20:06,673 --> 00:20:08,300
<i>Minus fifteen seconds.</i>

105
00:20:16,266 --> 00:20:18,769
<i>Minus ten seconds.</i>

106
00:20:19,019 --> 00:20:25,108
<i>Nine, eight, seven, six,</i>

107
00:20:25,192 --> 00:20:27,944
<i>five, four,</i>

108
00:20:28,028 --> 00:20:29,112
LEAD LINED

109
00:20:29,196 --> 00:20:33,366
<i>three, two, one, zero.</i>

110
00:22:04,291 --> 00:22:07,377
<i>I had no reason to think
Mac was a spy.</i>

111
00:22:07,460 --> 00:22:09,796
He was in MI6
when I was in the OSS.

112
00:22:09,880 --> 00:22:12,757
We did twenty or thirty missions.

113
00:22:12,841 --> 00:22:15,260
You don't boast about your military record.

114
00:22:15,343 --> 00:22:17,262
We all served.

115
00:22:17,345 --> 00:22:19,723
But which side were you on?

116
00:22:19,806 --> 00:22:22,559
I don't think you understand
the seriousness of your situation.

117
00:22:22,642 --> 00:22:25,145
You aided KGB agents...

118
00:22:25,228 --> 00:22:28,398
...who entered a military installation...

119
00:22:28,481 --> 00:22:31,276
...in the United States, my country.

120
00:22:31,359 --> 00:22:34,321
What was in the steel box?

121
00:22:34,404 --> 00:22:37,157
You tell us. You've seen it before.

122
00:22:41,578 --> 00:22:44,289
You're talking about the Air Force fiasco
back in '47.

123
00:22:46,416 --> 00:22:48,627
They put me on a bus...

124
00:22:48,710 --> 00:22:51,630
...with twenty guys
I wasn't allowed to talk to.

125
00:22:51,713 --> 00:22:53,798
They took me to the middle of nowhere
at midnight,

126
00:22:53,882 --> 00:22:56,426
...to see what?

127
00:22:56,509 --> 00:22:57,594
Fragments of an object...

128
00:22:57,677 --> 00:23:02,349
...and a highly magnetic shroud
covering mutilated remains?

129
00:23:02,432 --> 00:23:05,352
No one was given the full story.

130
00:23:05,435 --> 00:23:08,104
We were threatened with treason
if we ever spoke about it.

131
00:23:08,188 --> 00:23:10,023
What was in the box?

132
00:23:11,524 --> 00:23:13,777
It's dangerous
to climb into a refrigerator.

133
00:23:13,860 --> 00:23:15,612
They're death traps.

134
00:23:15,695 --> 00:23:18,114
Good to see you too, Bob.

135
00:23:18,198 --> 00:23:20,617
Easy, gentlemen. I vouch for Dr. Jones.

136
00:23:20,700 --> 00:23:22,577
What the hell is going on?

137
00:23:22,661 --> 00:23:25,372
The KGB on American soil?
Who is that woman?

138
00:23:25,455 --> 00:23:27,248
Describe her.

139
00:23:27,332 --> 00:23:28,959
Tall, thin, in her thirties.

140
00:23:29,042 --> 00:23:31,753
She had a sword, a rapier, I think.

141
00:23:35,382 --> 00:23:37,217
Yes, that's her.

142
00:23:37,300 --> 00:23:38,885
Are you sure she's here?

143
00:23:38,969 --> 00:23:41,346
She was here. Who is she?

144
00:23:41,429 --> 00:23:45,016
Irina Spalko, Stalin's right-hand woman.

145
00:23:45,100 --> 00:23:48,979
His favorite scientist,
if parapsychology is a science.

146
00:23:49,062 --> 00:23:50,146
General Ross.

147
00:23:50,230 --> 00:23:52,482
She runs teams all over the world,

148
00:23:52,565 --> 00:23:53,692
collecting material...

149
00:23:53,775 --> 00:23:56,486
...for paranormal
military applications.

150
00:23:56,569 --> 00:23:59,531
- General Ross!
- That's enough, Paul.

151
00:23:59,614 --> 00:24:03,159
Not everyone in the military is a Communist,
and Indy certainly isn't.

152
00:24:03,243 --> 00:24:07,664
What am I accused of, besides
surviving a nuclear explosion?

153
00:24:07,747 --> 00:24:08,832
Nothing.

154
00:24:08,915 --> 00:24:10,834
But your association with George McHale...

155
00:24:10,917 --> 00:24:15,171
...casts suspicion on all your activities,
including your wartime service.

156
00:24:15,255 --> 00:24:17,173
Are you crazy?

157
00:24:17,257 --> 00:24:20,510
Do you know how many medals
he's earned?

158
00:24:20,593 --> 00:24:22,595
Many, I'm sure.

159
00:24:23,763 --> 00:24:26,349
But did he deserve them?

160
00:24:26,433 --> 00:24:30,437
Dr. Jones, let's just say
the FBI is interested in you.

161
00:24:30,520 --> 00:24:32,689
Very interested.

162
00:24:41,573 --> 00:24:44,325
Corrugated pottery
in the earliest drains,

163
00:24:44,409 --> 00:24:47,871
which we also see at Skara Brae
in Scotland.

164
00:24:47,954 --> 00:24:50,582
Skara Brae dates back to 3100 BC...

165
00:24:50,665 --> 00:24:53,376
...and was occupied continuously
for 600 years...

166
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:56,337
...until it was abandoned in 2500 BC.

167
00:24:56,421 --> 00:24:58,548
There's no clear evidence
of why they decided...

168
00:24:58,631 --> 00:25:03,470
...to abandon such a healthy environment. Yes?

169
00:25:03,553 --> 00:25:06,973
May I have a moment?

170
00:25:07,640 --> 00:25:09,517
Open your Michaelson textbooks.
Review chapter four.

171
00:25:09,601 --> 00:25:10,727
Be prepared to discuss...

172
00:25:10,810 --> 00:25:13,772
...the difference between migration and exodus.

173
00:25:19,903 --> 00:25:21,154
What?

174
00:25:21,863 --> 00:25:24,657
The board of trustees
is putting pressure on me.

175
00:25:24,741 --> 00:25:26,076
The FBI came in,

176
00:25:26,159 --> 00:25:28,745
ransacked your office
and searched all your files!

177
00:25:28,828 --> 00:25:30,663
Why didn't you stop them?

178
00:25:30,747 --> 00:25:31,998
They had no right!

179
00:25:32,082 --> 00:25:34,501
They weren't vandals!

180
00:25:34,584 --> 00:25:37,003
They were federal agents
with search warrants!

181
00:25:37,087 --> 00:25:40,548
The university doesn't want trouble,

182
00:25:40,632 --> 00:25:42,759
not in this political climate.

183
00:25:43,802 --> 00:25:46,221
So you're firing me.

184
00:25:46,304 --> 00:25:50,600
We're only giving you leave,
an indefinite leave of absence.

185
00:25:50,683 --> 00:25:52,227
You are firing me!

186
00:25:52,310 --> 00:25:55,146
They're going to keep paying your salary.

187
00:25:55,230 --> 00:25:56,689
I don't want their money!

188
00:25:56,773 --> 00:25:59,275
Don't be foolish.

189
00:25:59,359 --> 00:26:01,277
You don't know what it cost me
to get you that.

190
00:26:01,361 --> 00:26:04,614
What it cost you?
What did it cost you, Charlie?

191
00:26:04,697 --> 00:26:06,074
Henry...

192
00:26:08,201 --> 00:26:10,078
I resigned.

193
00:26:17,794 --> 00:26:19,462
Where are you going?

194
00:26:20,922 --> 00:26:24,592
I'm taking a train to New York
and a flight to London.

195
00:26:25,718 --> 00:26:30,014
Maybe I'll end up teaching in Leipzig.
Heinrich owes me a favor.

196
00:26:30,098 --> 00:26:31,474
I'll call you when I'm settled.

197
00:26:31,558 --> 00:26:33,309
You can send me
the rest of my things.

198
00:26:33,393 --> 00:26:35,520
There's nothing
that can keep you here.

199
00:26:35,603 --> 00:26:37,564
I hardly recognize this country anymore.

200
00:26:37,647 --> 00:26:40,733
The government has us seeing
Communists everywhere.

201
00:26:40,817 --> 00:26:45,363
When hysteria reaches
the universities, it's time to quit.

202
00:26:45,446 --> 00:26:47,490
How did Deirdre take the news?

203
00:26:47,574 --> 00:26:49,868
How does a wife take something like that?

204
00:26:49,951 --> 00:26:53,121
There was a mixture
of pride and panic on her face.

205
00:26:53,204 --> 00:26:56,166
I was wrong to doubt you, my friend.

206
00:26:56,249 --> 00:27:00,003
There are reasons to question
your friends these days.

207
00:27:04,591 --> 00:27:07,051
It's been a brutal two years, Charlie.

208
00:27:09,012 --> 00:27:11,723
First Dad, then Marcus.

209
00:27:12,765 --> 00:27:15,185
We've reached an age
when life stops giving...

210
00:27:15,268 --> 00:27:17,437
...and starts taking away.

211
00:27:20,607 --> 00:27:22,775
Maybe another little half-glass.

212
00:28:24,003 --> 00:28:25,505
Hey, old man!

213
00:28:26,673 --> 00:28:30,718
Professor! Hello! Are you Dr. Jones?

214
00:28:30,802 --> 00:28:32,595
You're running out of platform, kid.

215
00:28:32,679 --> 00:28:34,889
Are you a friend of Dr. Oxley?

216
00:28:34,973 --> 00:28:37,350
Harold Oxley? The archaeologist?

217
00:28:37,433 --> 00:28:38,977
What about him?

218
00:28:40,019 --> 00:28:41,145
They're going to kill him!

219
00:28:43,898 --> 00:28:46,901
I haven't spoken to Harold Oxley
in twenty years.

220
00:28:48,278 --> 00:28:50,530
He's a brilliant man.

221
00:28:50,613 --> 00:28:53,491
Just listening to him put you to sleep.

222
00:28:53,574 --> 00:28:56,661
That's how I used to fall asleep as a kid.

223
00:28:56,744 --> 00:29:00,665
His voice worked better
than a glass of warm milk.

224
00:29:00,748 --> 00:29:02,709
I'm Mutt. Mutt Williams.

225
00:29:02,792 --> 00:29:05,962
Mutt? What kind of name is that?

226
00:29:06,045 --> 00:29:08,089
I chose it. Got a problem with it?

227
00:29:08,172 --> 00:29:12,468
Easy. What's your connection
to Oxley?

228
00:29:12,552 --> 00:29:17,056
My dad died in the war.
Ox helped my mom raise me.

229
00:29:17,140 --> 00:29:20,101
You said somebody is going to kill him?

230
00:29:22,770 --> 00:29:26,107
Six months ago,
my mom got a letter from Ox.

231
00:29:26,190 --> 00:29:27,650
He was in Peru.

232
00:29:27,734 --> 00:29:30,445
He'd found some kind
of crystal skull.

233
00:29:30,528 --> 00:29:34,157
Like the one Mitchell...
Hodgkiss? Hedgkiss? found?

234
00:29:34,240 --> 00:29:36,576
Like <i>that</i> one.

235
00:29:37,577 --> 00:29:41,998
Ox and I were obsessed
with that skull in college.

236
00:29:42,081 --> 00:29:43,249
How do you know about it?

237
00:29:43,333 --> 00:29:44,625
He used to talk about it for hours.

238
00:29:44,709 --> 00:29:48,504
What is it, some kind of idol?

239
00:29:48,588 --> 00:29:51,382
It's a sculpture
of a Mesoamerican deity.

240
00:29:51,466 --> 00:29:53,593
There are several crystal skulls.

241
00:29:53,676 --> 00:29:56,429
I saw one at the British Museum.

242
00:29:56,763 --> 00:30:00,391
It's an interesting piece of craftsmanship,
but nothing more.

243
00:30:00,475 --> 00:30:03,311
Laugh if you want.
Oxley said he had found it...

244
00:30:03,394 --> 00:30:07,357
...and was taking it
to a place called Akator.

245
00:30:07,440 --> 00:30:10,193
Akator? He said that? Are you sure?

246
00:30:10,276 --> 00:30:13,946
He said Akator. What's that?

247
00:30:14,030 --> 00:30:17,658
It's a mythical city
lost in the Amazon.

248
00:30:17,742 --> 00:30:20,703
The conquistadors called it
El Dorado.

249
00:30:20,787 --> 00:30:24,874
They say the Ugha were chosen
by the gods 7,000 years ago...

250
00:30:24,957 --> 00:30:28,711
...to build a gigantic city
of pure gold.

251
00:30:28,795 --> 00:30:31,381
It had aqueducts, paved streets...

252
00:30:31,464 --> 00:30:36,552
...and technology
not seen again for 5,000 years.

253
00:30:36,636 --> 00:30:41,641
Francisco de Orellana disappeared
searching for it in the Amazon in 1546.

254
00:30:42,642 --> 00:30:45,645
And I nearly died
of typhus searching for it.

255
00:30:47,146 --> 00:30:48,523
I don't think it exists.

256
00:30:48,606 --> 00:30:51,484
Why would he take the skull there?

257
00:30:51,567 --> 00:30:55,405
Legend says a crystal skull
was stolen from Akator...

258
00:30:55,488 --> 00:30:57,407
...in the fifteenth or sixteenth century,

259
00:30:57,490 --> 00:31:01,119
and whoever returns it to the temple...

260
00:31:01,202 --> 00:31:03,413
...will control its power.

261
00:31:03,496 --> 00:31:07,333
Power? What kind of power?

262
00:31:07,417 --> 00:31:10,503
I don't know, kid. It's just a story.

263
00:31:10,586 --> 00:31:11,754
No.

264
00:31:12,630 --> 00:31:14,757
My mom read the letter
and thought Ox had gone crazy.

265
00:31:14,841 --> 00:31:15,883
She went there to find him,

266
00:31:15,967 --> 00:31:19,262
but he'd been kidnapped.

267
00:31:19,345 --> 00:31:21,097
Now they've got her too.

268
00:31:21,180 --> 00:31:22,640
Ox said he'd hidden the skull...

269
00:31:22,723 --> 00:31:25,977
...and if she doesn't find it, they'll kill them.

270
00:31:26,060 --> 00:31:28,187
She said you'd help me.

271
00:31:28,938 --> 00:31:31,107
Me? What's your mom's name?

272
00:31:31,190 --> 00:31:33,943
Mary Williams. Remember her?

273
00:31:34,026 --> 00:31:35,278
I've known a lot of Marys.

274
00:31:35,361 --> 00:31:39,365
Shut up! You're talking about my mother!

275
00:31:39,449 --> 00:31:42,452
You don't have to get angry
to prove you're a man.

276
00:31:42,535 --> 00:31:44,704
Sit down.

277
00:31:46,038 --> 00:31:49,000
She said you could find
the skull.

278
00:31:49,083 --> 00:31:53,629
Like you were some kind of tomb robber
or something.

279
00:31:53,713 --> 00:31:56,549
I'm a university professor
of archaeology.

280
00:31:56,632 --> 00:32:00,887
Oh, you're a professor.
That's going to help us a lot.

281
00:32:00,970 --> 00:32:02,972
She called me from South America.

282
00:32:03,055 --> 00:32:05,558
She'd escaped,
but they were chasing her.

283
00:32:05,641 --> 00:32:07,310
She'd received a letter from Ox...

284
00:32:07,393 --> 00:32:09,395
...and sent it to me to give you.

285
00:32:09,479 --> 00:32:10,855
Then the line went dead. I opened it.

286
00:32:10,938 --> 00:32:13,399
It's full of scribbles.

287
00:32:13,483 --> 00:32:15,860
It's not even our alphabet.

288
00:32:15,943 --> 00:32:17,111
See?

289
00:32:18,946 --> 00:32:22,283
See those two goons
sitting at the bar?

290
00:32:22,366 --> 00:32:24,952
They're not here for milkshakes.

291
00:32:25,036 --> 00:32:26,537
Who are they?

292
00:32:27,330 --> 00:32:30,249
Maybe they're FBI.

293
00:32:30,333 --> 00:32:32,084
Come quietly, Dr. Jones.

294
00:32:32,168 --> 00:32:33,586
More likely KGB.

295
00:32:33,669 --> 00:32:36,756
- And bring the letter with you.
- What letter?

296
00:32:36,839 --> 00:32:38,174
The letter Mr. Williams gave you.

297
00:32:38,257 --> 00:32:40,176
Me? Do I look like a mailman?

298
00:32:40,259 --> 00:32:42,011
Come now or...

299
00:32:42,094 --> 00:32:43,262
Or what?

300
00:32:43,346 --> 00:32:46,599
You tried, but I think you've brought
a knife...

301
00:32:47,934 --> 00:32:50,186
...to a gunfight.

302
00:32:50,269 --> 00:32:51,938
Outside. Now.

303
00:32:56,359 --> 00:32:57,527
- Hit him.
- Who?

304
00:32:57,610 --> 00:32:59,070
The college boy.

305
00:32:59,153 --> 00:33:01,113
Hold this for me.

306
00:33:02,114 --> 00:33:03,950
That's my boyfriend!

307
00:33:08,955 --> 00:33:10,790
Get that hoodlum!

308
00:33:18,506 --> 00:33:19,924
Your mother didn't escape!

309
00:33:20,007 --> 00:33:22,802
They let her go
so she'd send the letter...

310
00:33:22,885 --> 00:33:26,138
...and you'd have me translate it!

311
00:33:28,307 --> 00:33:30,142
Get on, Grandpa!

312
00:33:58,588 --> 00:34:00,923
Hurry, hurry!

313
00:35:10,660 --> 00:35:14,413
Better dead than Red!
Better dead than Red!

314
00:35:25,091 --> 00:35:28,594
This is crazy!
You're going to hurt somebody!

315
00:35:28,678 --> 00:35:30,221
BETTER DEAD THAN RED!

316
00:35:56,580 --> 00:35:58,249
To the left!

317
00:36:13,431 --> 00:36:15,224
Out of the way!

318
00:36:17,309 --> 00:36:19,729
- You're going too fast!
- That's what you think!

319
00:36:32,867 --> 00:36:35,703
Excuse me, Dr. Jones.

320
00:36:35,786 --> 00:36:39,123
I had a question about
Hargrove's model of culture.

321
00:36:39,206 --> 00:36:40,583
Forget Hargrove.

322
00:36:40,666 --> 00:36:43,753
Read Gordon Childe
on diffusionism.

323
00:36:43,836 --> 00:36:46,172
He spent most of his life
in the field.

324
00:36:47,006 --> 00:36:50,009
If you want to be a good archaeologist,

325
00:36:51,135 --> 00:36:53,679
you've got to get out of the library!

326
00:36:58,476 --> 00:36:59,518
Who's that?

327
00:36:59,602 --> 00:37:02,730
Francisco de Orellana, a conquistador.

328
00:37:02,813 --> 00:37:05,065
The one who disappeared looking for the skull.

329
00:37:05,149 --> 00:37:08,652
Just what I thought.

330
00:37:08,736 --> 00:37:10,738
Koihoma.

331
00:37:10,821 --> 00:37:12,281
What's that?

332
00:37:12,364 --> 00:37:17,369
An extinct South American language
with a pre-Columbian syllabic base.

333
00:37:18,621 --> 00:37:22,666
Diagonal strokes above
the ideograms. Definitely Koihoma.

334
00:37:22,750 --> 00:37:24,627
- You speak it?
- Nobody does.

335
00:37:24,710 --> 00:37:28,047
Nobody's heard it in 3,000 years.

336
00:37:28,130 --> 00:37:32,384
I might be able to read a little
using Mayan as a guide.

337
00:37:33,219 --> 00:37:35,679
You fight pretty well for an old man.

338
00:37:35,763 --> 00:37:37,515
Thank you very much.

339
00:37:37,598 --> 00:37:39,558
What are you, eighty?

340
00:37:42,937 --> 00:37:45,105
It's a riddle.

341
00:37:45,189 --> 00:37:48,734
Typical Ox, writing a riddle
in a dead language.

342
00:37:50,236 --> 00:37:54,323
"Follow the lines of the earth
that only the gods can read.

343
00:37:54,406 --> 00:37:57,493
They lead to Orellana's cradle.

344
00:37:57,576 --> 00:37:59,829
Protected by the living dead..."

345
00:37:59,912 --> 00:38:02,832
- It means the <i>Nazca</i> lines.
- What are those?

346
00:38:02,915 --> 00:38:04,250
Wait.

347
00:38:05,042 --> 00:38:06,126
Geoglyphs.

348
00:38:06,210 --> 00:38:11,507
Giant ancient drawings
etched into the Peruvian desert.

349
00:38:11,590 --> 00:38:14,343
You can't see anything from the ground.

350
00:38:14,426 --> 00:38:18,055
But from the sky,
only the gods can read them,

351
00:38:18,138 --> 00:38:20,975
because only the gods live up there.

352
00:38:22,935 --> 00:38:28,190
Oxley is telling us
the skull is in Nazca, Peru.

353
00:39:30,002 --> 00:39:32,129
Finally. They saw him.

354
00:39:32,212 --> 00:39:35,090
Ox arrived two months ago.
He seemed insane.

355
00:39:35,174 --> 00:39:37,593
They put him in an asylum.

356
00:39:37,676 --> 00:39:40,095
I studied Spanish
and I didn't understand a word.

357
00:39:40,179 --> 00:39:42,389
It's Quechua, a local Inca dialect.

358
00:39:42,473 --> 00:39:45,059
- Where did you learn it?
- It's a long story.

359
00:39:45,142 --> 00:39:46,727
I've got time.

360
00:39:46,810 --> 00:39:49,229
I rode with Pancho Villa.
His men spoke it.

361
00:39:49,313 --> 00:39:50,981
You're lying!

362
00:39:51,065 --> 00:39:53,317
- You asked.
- Pancho Villa?

363
00:39:53,400 --> 00:39:56,570
- Technically, I was kidnapped.
- By Pancho Villa?

364
00:39:56,654 --> 00:40:00,115
He was fighting
against Victoriano Huerta.

365
00:40:00,199 --> 00:40:02,409
- How old were you?
- About your age.

366
00:40:02,493 --> 00:40:04,995
Your parents must've had a fit, huh?

367
00:40:05,079 --> 00:40:07,247
Things were tense at <i>home</i>.

368
00:40:07,331 --> 00:40:09,208
Yeah, like between my mom and me.

369
00:40:09,291 --> 00:40:12,753
Treat her well. You only have one,
and sometimes not for very long.

370
00:40:14,213 --> 00:40:15,839
She was mad because I quit school.

371
00:40:15,923 --> 00:40:17,424
She thinks I'm a bum.

372
00:40:17,508 --> 00:40:19,718
- You quit school?
- Yeah. Lots of them.

373
00:40:19,802 --> 00:40:21,971
They were for rich kids,
with debating classes...

374
00:40:22,054 --> 00:40:23,722
...and fencing.

375
00:40:23,806 --> 00:40:25,975
I'm good at fencing,
but it's a waste of time.

376
00:40:26,058 --> 00:40:28,143
- You never finished?
- No.

377
00:40:28,227 --> 00:40:30,854
They only taught useless things
from boring books.

378
00:40:30,938 --> 00:40:33,315
I love reading.
Ox and I used to read a lot.

379
00:40:33,399 --> 00:40:35,818
But now I choose my own books.

380
00:40:35,901 --> 00:40:38,529
- How do you earn a living?
- I fix motorcycles.

381
00:40:38,612 --> 00:40:41,323
You going to do that the rest of your life?

382
00:40:41,407 --> 00:40:43,993
Maybe, teach. Got a problem with that?

383
00:40:44,076 --> 00:40:46,495
Not if that's what you enjoy doing.

384
00:40:46,578 --> 00:40:49,456
Don't let anyone talk you out of it.

385
00:40:49,540 --> 00:40:51,375
This is it. Sister.

386
00:41:11,228 --> 00:41:14,648
She says Ox isn't here.
She doesn't know where he is.

387
00:41:15,816 --> 00:41:19,778
Some men came and took him away.
Men with guns.

388
00:41:25,117 --> 00:41:27,036
She says he was insane.

389
00:41:27,119 --> 00:41:29,621
He drew on the walls of his cell.

390
00:41:33,792 --> 00:41:37,212
The riddle in Oxley's letter
doesn't make sense.

391
00:41:37,296 --> 00:41:42,968
"Follow the lines the gods can read.
They lead to Orellana's cradle."

392
00:41:43,052 --> 00:41:45,345
Cradle, cradle, birthplace.

393
00:41:45,429 --> 00:41:48,432
Orellana wasn't born in Peru,
but in Spain.

394
00:41:48,515 --> 00:41:50,434
He came for the gold.

395
00:41:50,517 --> 00:41:51,935
What happened to him?

396
00:41:52,019 --> 00:41:56,273
He disappeared with six other men.
Their bodies were never found.

397
00:42:25,511 --> 00:42:27,721
He must've lost his mind.

398
00:42:34,353 --> 00:42:37,731
Ox, my friend, what happened to you?
What happened?

399
00:42:45,447 --> 00:42:48,575
This isn't the Mitchell-Hedges skull.

400
00:42:48,659 --> 00:42:50,994
Look at the elongated cranium.

401
00:42:51,787 --> 00:42:53,831
And the same word...

402
00:42:53,914 --> 00:42:56,166
...in different languages, over and over.

403
00:43:00,546 --> 00:43:02,297
Return. Go back.

404
00:43:02,381 --> 00:43:04,133
Go back where?

405
00:43:04,216 --> 00:43:06,260
Or return what?

406
00:43:06,343 --> 00:43:08,846
Does it mean the skull?

407
00:43:08,929 --> 00:43:11,473
He was obsessed with it.

408
00:43:11,557 --> 00:43:14,226
Where did he need to return it?

409
00:43:26,572 --> 00:43:28,198
Sweep.

410
00:43:37,791 --> 00:43:40,460
He didn't mean the place
where Orellana was born.

411
00:43:40,544 --> 00:43:42,963
"Cradle" has another meaning in Mayan.

412
00:43:43,046 --> 00:43:48,010
It literally means "resting place,"
as in "final resting place."

413
00:43:48,093 --> 00:43:50,512
He meant Orellana's tomb.

414
00:43:50,596 --> 00:43:55,642
This drawing he made on the floor
is the cemetery where he's buried.

415
00:43:56,101 --> 00:43:58,687
You said Orellana disappeared
and no one ever found him.

416
00:43:58,770 --> 00:44:02,608
Apparently Harold Oxley did.

417
00:44:33,472 --> 00:44:35,557
"Grave robbers will be shot."

418
00:44:35,641 --> 00:44:37,601
Good thing we're not thieves.

419
00:44:45,567 --> 00:44:48,779
- What are we looking for?
- I don't know yet.

420
00:44:49,279 --> 00:44:52,741
Maybe an antechamber
in one of these mounds.

421
00:45:08,507 --> 00:45:10,092
I just saw something!

422
00:45:10,175 --> 00:45:12,511
You're letting the shadows scare you.

423
00:45:17,140 --> 00:45:19,101
This way down.

424
00:45:27,943 --> 00:45:30,570
This way up.

425
00:45:54,845 --> 00:45:56,763
Those darts are poisoned!

426
00:45:58,432 --> 00:45:59,933
Stay there.

427
00:46:24,333 --> 00:46:27,002
You're a professor?

428
00:46:28,170 --> 00:46:29,588
Part-time.

429
00:46:39,514 --> 00:46:40,849
It <i>ends</i> here.

430
00:46:42,934 --> 00:46:44,436
Maybe.

431
00:46:50,817 --> 00:46:52,069
What are you doing? Put that away.

432
00:46:52,152 --> 00:46:54,613
And shine the light over here.

433
00:47:00,535 --> 00:47:02,371
Bring it over here.

434
00:47:09,294 --> 00:47:11,046
It's nothing.

435
00:48:11,106 --> 00:48:12,649
Dance on your own time.

436
00:48:12,732 --> 00:48:14,776
A scorpion stung me!
Am I going to die?

437
00:48:14,860 --> 00:48:16,987
- How big was it?
- Huge!

438
00:48:17,070 --> 00:48:18,613
That's good.

439
00:48:20,073 --> 00:48:22,951
With scorpions,
the bigger the better.

440
00:48:23,034 --> 00:48:26,455
If a little one stings you,
let me know right away.

441
00:48:47,309 --> 00:48:51,438
Their skulls. Look at their skulls.

442
00:48:51,521 --> 00:48:55,150
They're like Oxley's drawings.
We're getting closer.

443
00:48:55,233 --> 00:48:57,319
Why are they like that?

444
00:48:57,402 --> 00:49:00,822
The <i>Nazca</i> people bound
their babies' heads...

445
00:49:00,906 --> 00:49:02,532
...to elongate their skulls.

446
00:49:02,616 --> 00:49:05,243
- Why?
- To honor their gods.

447
00:49:05,327 --> 00:49:08,580
No. God's head doesn't look like that.

448
00:49:08,663 --> 00:49:10,790
It depends on which god you worship.

449
00:49:20,842 --> 00:49:22,928
It goes nowhere.

450
00:49:27,891 --> 00:49:30,393
There's no way out. Look!

451
00:49:55,877 --> 00:49:58,964
Come on, genius. Bring the backpack.

452
00:50:16,565 --> 00:50:19,359
- This is incredible.
- It's unreal.

453
00:50:22,153 --> 00:50:24,322
Don't touch anything.

454
00:50:27,242 --> 00:50:29,661
Footprints. Somebody was here.

455
00:50:31,204 --> 00:50:32,664
Recently.

456
00:50:35,709 --> 00:50:37,377
Two sets of footprints.

457
00:50:38,795 --> 00:50:42,591
The same size.
Maybe the same person twice.

458
00:50:42,674 --> 00:50:44,259
Not bad, kid.

459
00:50:52,183 --> 00:50:55,812
One, two...

460
00:50:55,895 --> 00:50:59,274
...three, four, five, six...

461
00:51:01,026 --> 00:51:02,402
...seven.

462
00:51:03,111 --> 00:51:07,198
Orellana and his men might
have made it out of the jungle.

463
00:51:09,576 --> 00:51:11,411
Bring the light closer.

464
00:51:17,542 --> 00:51:19,961
You wouldn't happen to have a knife, would you?

465
00:51:43,234 --> 00:51:45,028
He looks like he died yesterday.

466
00:51:45,111 --> 00:51:47,864
The wrappings preserved him.

467
00:51:56,456 --> 00:51:57,499
What happened?

468
00:51:57,582 --> 00:52:01,544
He's been wrapped up for 500 years.
The air is damaging him.

469
00:52:09,219 --> 00:52:10,595
Thanks.

470
00:52:14,933 --> 00:52:16,810
I didn't want to have to ask for it.

471
00:52:16,893 --> 00:52:19,896
- It's okay.
- I was going to give it back.

472
00:52:25,610 --> 00:52:27,654
Has this been opened already?

473
00:52:46,715 --> 00:52:49,217
It's him.

474
00:52:49,384 --> 00:52:51,636
It's Orellana himself.

475
00:52:56,599 --> 00:53:00,937
They called him the Golden Man.
His thirst for gold was legendary.

476
00:53:02,647 --> 00:53:04,023
How strange.

477
00:53:06,776 --> 00:53:09,195
Someone came and went.

478
00:53:09,279 --> 00:53:15,326
But they left all this gold
and all these artifacts.

479
00:53:17,370 --> 00:53:19,581
What were they looking for?

480
00:53:32,677 --> 00:53:34,471
Hold this.

481
00:53:53,907 --> 00:53:57,035
Incredible.

482
00:54:05,835 --> 00:54:07,670
There are no tool marks.

483
00:54:07,754 --> 00:54:10,381
A single piece of flawless quartz.

484
00:54:12,175 --> 00:54:14,803
Carved against the grain.

485
00:54:14,886 --> 00:54:19,015
It's impossible,
even with today's technology.

486
00:54:25,522 --> 00:54:27,565
Crystal isn't magnetic.

487
00:54:27,649 --> 00:54:29,234
Neither is gold.

488
00:54:33,112 --> 00:54:35,782
What is this thing?

489
00:54:35,865 --> 00:54:39,994
Maybe the <i>Nazca</i> Indians believed
this was their god.

490
00:54:40,078 --> 00:54:42,914
- Do you think this is the one from...?
- Akator.

491
00:54:47,585 --> 00:54:52,173
Perhaps the Spanish found
this skull with the other treasure.

492
00:54:52,257 --> 00:54:55,677
But they were heading for their ships
on the coast.

493
00:54:57,095 --> 00:55:00,056
Or perhaps the Indians caught up with them...

494
00:55:00,139 --> 00:55:02,684
...or they fought among themselves...

495
00:55:02,767 --> 00:55:06,229
...over the treasure and killed each other.

496
00:55:06,312 --> 00:55:09,566
The Indians wrapped and buried them.

497
00:55:09,649 --> 00:55:14,737
A couple hundred years later,
Oxley arrives and discovers the skull.

498
00:55:14,821 --> 00:55:18,449
He takes it, maybe to Akator.

499
00:55:21,119 --> 00:55:23,621
But then he brings it back here.

500
00:55:23,705 --> 00:55:25,415
He returns it.

501
00:55:25,498 --> 00:55:28,668
"Return." That's what he wrote in his cell.

502
00:55:29,460 --> 00:55:33,631
He brought it back where he found it.
Why?

503
00:55:57,655 --> 00:56:01,284
- Hello, Jonesy.
- Hello, <i>Mac</i>.

504
00:56:34,067 --> 00:56:35,944
You're lucky I came.

505
00:56:36,027 --> 00:56:38,279
Dovchenko wanted to blow your brains out.

506
00:56:38,363 --> 00:56:41,908
That's the third time I've saved your life.

507
00:56:41,991 --> 00:56:44,118
Take off the handcuffs
and I'll give you a hug.

508
00:56:44,202 --> 00:56:47,121
You had a Luger pointed at your head.

509
00:56:47,205 --> 00:56:48,957
When we first met.

510
00:56:49,040 --> 00:56:50,917
I had the situation under control.

511
00:56:51,000 --> 00:56:53,711
- You owe me one.
- What do you owe them?

512
00:56:55,838 --> 00:56:57,048
After the war,

513
00:56:57,131 --> 00:56:58,299
how many names did you give them?

514
00:56:58,383 --> 00:56:59,759
There were a lot of factors!

515
00:56:59,842 --> 00:57:01,844
How many men died?

516
00:57:01,928 --> 00:57:04,222
One day they'll let me go, comrade.

517
00:57:04,305 --> 00:57:07,684
And when they do,
I'm going to break your nose.

518
00:57:07,767 --> 00:57:11,104
"Comrade"? You think this is
about flags?

519
00:57:11,187 --> 00:57:13,564
About uniforms?
About lines on a map?

520
00:57:13,648 --> 00:57:15,441
It's just about money, isn't it?

521
00:57:15,525 --> 00:57:17,318
No, not just money.

522
00:57:20,405 --> 00:57:22,824
A huge amount of money.

523
00:57:23,574 --> 00:57:25,994
What the Russians are paying me...

524
00:57:26,077 --> 00:57:29,080
...is nothing compared
to what's in Akator.

525
00:57:30,289 --> 00:57:34,669
A whole city of gold!
What the conquistadors were after!

526
00:57:35,920 --> 00:57:40,591
For God's sake! We'll be
richer than Howard Hughes!

527
00:57:40,675 --> 00:57:43,177
With blood money.

528
00:57:43,261 --> 00:57:45,471
I need you to look at this differently.

529
00:57:45,555 --> 00:57:48,433
Wise up. Like in...

530
00:57:50,268 --> 00:57:52,854
Just like in Berlin, understand?

531
00:57:52,937 --> 00:57:55,023
Just like Berlin.

532
00:57:55,106 --> 00:57:58,359
Do you want me to get lost
or shall we sing a song?

533
00:57:59,527 --> 00:58:03,322
We're lucky we didn't kill you,
Dr. Jones.

534
00:58:03,406 --> 00:58:06,409
You survived to be useful to us again.

535
00:58:06,492 --> 00:58:09,454
You know me,
always happy to help.

536
00:58:09,537 --> 00:58:14,125
"Now I am become Death,
the destroyer of worlds."

537
00:58:14,959 --> 00:58:17,712
Do you recognize those words?

538
00:58:17,795 --> 00:58:22,216
Dr. Oppenheimer said them
after creating the atomic bomb.

539
00:58:22,300 --> 00:58:23,718
He was quoting the Hindu scriptures.

540
00:58:23,801 --> 00:58:25,887
You intimidated us with the nuclear bomb.

541
00:58:25,970 --> 00:58:29,974
Now we'll have
this new weapon...

542
00:58:30,058 --> 00:58:31,976
...and you will fear us.

543
00:58:32,060 --> 00:58:34,562
A weapon? What weapon?

544
00:58:34,645 --> 00:58:36,397
A mental weapon.

545
00:58:36,481 --> 00:58:40,318
The ultimate in
parapsychological warfare. Stalin's dream.

546
00:58:42,987 --> 00:58:47,033
Now I know why Oxley returned
the skull to where he found it.

547
00:58:47,116 --> 00:58:48,367
He knew you were looking for it.

548
00:58:48,451 --> 00:58:50,453
It's not a representation of a god.

549
00:58:50,536 --> 00:58:52,580
Surely you realized that when you saw it.

550
00:58:52,663 --> 00:58:55,708
It wasn't made by human beings.

551
00:58:55,792 --> 00:58:57,543
Then who made it?

552
00:59:00,797 --> 00:59:02,298
Please.

553
00:59:03,716 --> 00:59:06,594
The body from New Mexico
wasn't the first.

554
00:59:06,677 --> 00:59:10,556
We dissected two others
from sites in the Soviet Union.

555
00:59:10,640 --> 00:59:12,391
Martians in flying saucers.

556
00:59:12,475 --> 00:59:14,602
The legends of Akator are true.

557
00:59:14,685 --> 00:59:17,105
Humans could never
have built it,

558
00:59:17,188 --> 00:59:19,357
a city of superior beings...

559
00:59:19,440 --> 00:59:22,527
...with technology
and paranormal abilities.

560
00:59:22,610 --> 00:59:24,987
You must be joking.

561
00:59:25,071 --> 00:59:28,366
Why do you refuse to believe
what you see with your own eyes?

562
00:59:30,868 --> 00:59:33,955
The New Mexico specimen
gave us encouragement.

563
00:59:34,038 --> 00:59:38,918
Unlike the others,
its skeleton was made of pure crystal.

564
00:59:40,711 --> 00:59:44,882
A distant cousin, perhaps,
sent to find Akator.

565
00:59:44,966 --> 00:59:48,052
Perhaps we're all looking
for the same thing.

566
00:59:50,513 --> 00:59:55,101
- There's no other explanation.
- There's always another explanation.

567
00:59:55,184 --> 00:59:59,438
The skull was stolen from Akator
in the fifteenth century.

568
00:59:59,522 --> 01:00:00,648
Whoever returns it...

569
01:00:00,731 --> 01:00:03,151
...to the temple will gain control of its power.

570
01:00:03,234 --> 01:00:06,195
I've heard that story already.

571
01:00:06,279 --> 01:00:09,323
It's a legend.
Why do you believe Akator exists?

572
01:00:09,407 --> 01:00:11,826
Ask your friend.

573
01:00:11,909 --> 01:00:14,203
We know he's been there.

574
01:00:15,246 --> 01:00:16,455
Oxley?

575
01:00:31,179 --> 01:00:33,514
Ox, it's me, Indy.

576
01:00:34,348 --> 01:00:35,850
Ox?

577
01:00:37,393 --> 01:00:39,812
Ox, you're pretending, aren't you?

578
01:00:39,896 --> 01:00:42,815
"Through eyes that I saw crying."

579
01:00:42,899 --> 01:00:46,903
Listen, old friend.
Your name is Harold Oxley.

580
01:00:46,986 --> 01:00:48,446
You were born in Leeds, England.

581
01:00:48,529 --> 01:00:51,282
We studied at the University of Chicago.

582
01:00:51,365 --> 01:00:54,619
And you were never this interesting.
My name is In...

583
01:00:56,120 --> 01:00:58,789
My name is Henry Jones, Junior.

584
01:01:00,166 --> 01:01:01,292
What did you do to him?

585
01:01:01,375 --> 01:01:03,628
Nothing. It's the skull.

586
01:01:03,711 --> 01:01:06,923
He's our magic wand
that will lead us to Akator.

587
01:01:07,006 --> 01:01:09,425
We need someone to interpret.

588
01:01:09,508 --> 01:01:14,222
His mind is weak.
Let's hope yours is stronger.

589
01:01:16,974 --> 01:01:21,312
The crystal stimulates
an undeveloped part of the brain,

590
01:01:21,395 --> 01:01:23,564
opening a psychic channel.

591
01:01:23,648 --> 01:01:27,735
Oxley lost control of his mind
by staring into its eyes too long.

592
01:01:27,818 --> 01:01:32,406
We believe you can communicate
with it by doing the same thing.

593
01:01:32,490 --> 01:01:35,243
I've got a better idea. You look at it.

594
01:01:35,326 --> 01:01:38,454
The skull doesn't seem
to speak to just anyone.

595
01:01:39,830 --> 01:01:42,416
Are you sure you're not afraid,
Dr. Jones?

596
01:01:42,500 --> 01:01:45,086
You've spent your life
searching for answers.

597
01:01:45,169 --> 01:01:48,256
Think of the truth hidden
behind those eyes.

598
01:01:48,339 --> 01:01:50,925
There could be hundreds of skulls
in Akator.

599
01:01:51,008 --> 01:01:53,594
Whoever finds them will control...

600
01:01:53,678 --> 01:01:55,846
...the greatest natural force known.

601
01:01:58,182 --> 01:02:00,726
Power over the human mind.

602
01:02:00,810 --> 01:02:04,438
Be careful, you may get what you wish for.

603
01:02:04,522 --> 01:02:06,148
I usually do.

604
01:02:39,223 --> 01:02:41,642
Just imagine.

605
01:02:41,726 --> 01:02:45,396
Looking across the world
and knowing your enemy's secrets.

606
01:02:45,479 --> 01:02:48,733
Planting our ideas
in the minds of your leaders.

607
01:02:48,816 --> 01:02:51,402
Your teachers will teach the real history...

608
01:02:51,485 --> 01:02:54,196
...and your soldiers will obey us.

609
01:02:54,405 --> 01:02:57,199
We'll be everywhere at once,
all-powerful.

610
01:02:57,283 --> 01:02:58,659
Invading your dreams,

611
01:02:58,743 --> 01:03:01,912
thinking for you while you sleep.

612
01:03:03,789 --> 01:03:06,667
We will change you, Dr. Jones.

613
01:03:06,751 --> 01:03:09,545
All of you, from within.

614
01:03:09,628 --> 01:03:12,340
We will turn you into us.

615
01:03:12,423 --> 01:03:17,261
And the best part? You won't even know
it's happening.

616
01:03:19,597 --> 01:03:20,890
Return.

617
01:03:23,768 --> 01:03:25,102
Return?

618
01:03:28,481 --> 01:03:30,024
Enough.

619
01:03:32,777 --> 01:03:35,863
That's enough.
If he dies, we can't get there.

620
01:03:37,073 --> 01:03:38,407
Cover it.

621
01:03:43,579 --> 01:03:44,955
Henry.

622
01:03:45,664 --> 01:03:47,750
There you go, Jonesy.

623
01:03:53,464 --> 01:03:54,882
- You broke my nose!
- I warned you.

624
01:03:54,965 --> 01:03:59,678
Enough! You'll talk to Oxley
and lead us to Akator, yes?

625
01:03:59,762 --> 01:04:01,472
<i>Nyet.</i>

626
01:04:01,555 --> 01:04:03,307
Take him outside.

627
01:04:09,188 --> 01:04:11,816
- Are you okay, kid?
- They left my bike at the cemetery.

628
01:04:11,899 --> 01:04:13,734
- But are you okay?
- They left my bike.

629
01:04:13,818 --> 01:04:17,279
Wait, wait! Stop!

630
01:04:25,538 --> 01:04:29,417
I'm ready.
Don't tell those pigs anything.

631
01:04:29,500 --> 01:04:31,085
You heard him.

632
01:04:31,168 --> 01:04:34,672
Obviously I've been pressing
the wrong spot.

633
01:04:34,755 --> 01:04:37,550
Perhaps I can find
a more sensitive one.

634
01:04:39,009 --> 01:04:43,305
Get your hands off me,
you filthy Russian! Son of a bitch!

635
01:04:46,517 --> 01:04:48,352
Indiana Jones.

636
01:04:52,273 --> 01:04:53,649
It's about time.

637
01:04:53,732 --> 01:04:55,609
- Mom!
- Sweetheart!

638
01:04:55,693 --> 01:04:57,361
"Mom"?

639
01:04:57,445 --> 01:04:58,779
What are you doing here?

640
01:04:58,863 --> 01:05:00,948
Forget about me. Are you okay?

641
01:05:01,031 --> 01:05:03,284
- "Mom"?
- I clearly told you...

642
01:05:03,367 --> 01:05:04,952
- ...not to come.
- Marion is your mom?

643
01:05:05,035 --> 01:05:07,079
You never told me.

644
01:05:07,163 --> 01:05:09,331
Marion Ravenwood is your mother?

645
01:05:09,415 --> 01:05:12,626
For God's sake,
it's not that hard to understand.

646
01:05:12,710 --> 01:05:14,628
It's just that I never imagined...

647
01:05:14,712 --> 01:05:16,297
...that I'd go on with my life without you.

648
01:05:16,380 --> 01:05:18,883
- That's not...
- And it's a good life!

649
01:05:18,966 --> 01:05:22,553
- All right.
- A wonderfully good life!

650
01:05:22,636 --> 01:05:24,138
Mine too!

651
01:05:24,221 --> 01:05:26,015
Are you still leaving a trail
of human misery...

652
01:05:26,098 --> 01:05:27,516
...or have you retired?

653
01:05:27,600 --> 01:05:29,101
Why? Are you looking for a partner?

654
01:05:29,185 --> 01:05:31,437
Anyone but you.

655
01:05:31,520 --> 01:05:35,065
So, Dr. Jones, will you help us?

656
01:05:35,149 --> 01:05:36,609
A simple "yes" will do.

657
01:05:36,692 --> 01:05:39,153
I don't know how you let yourself get kidnapped.

658
01:05:39,236 --> 01:05:41,405
You didn't do much better.

659
01:05:42,990 --> 01:05:44,783
Same old story.

660
01:05:46,994 --> 01:05:49,163
Henry Jones, Junior.

661
01:05:50,873 --> 01:05:54,376
- Henry Jones, Junior.
- That's right, Ox.

662
01:05:54,460 --> 01:05:57,463
"Place your hands together
upon the golden key...

663
01:05:57,546 --> 01:06:00,382
...that opens the Palace of Eternity."

664
01:06:00,466 --> 01:06:02,968
That's Milton. He's said it before.

665
01:06:03,052 --> 01:06:05,554
Ox, tell us how to get to Akator...

666
01:06:05,638 --> 01:06:07,056
...or they'll kill Marion.

667
01:06:07,139 --> 01:06:11,477
"Through eyes that I saw crying,
in the kingdom of dreams."

668
01:06:11,560 --> 01:06:14,104
Harold, they're going to kill Abner's girl.

669
01:06:14,188 --> 01:06:17,316
Tell us how to get there. We need...

670
01:06:25,157 --> 01:06:27,284
Give me some paper, something to write with.

671
01:06:38,128 --> 01:06:40,881
Automatic writing!
I should've figured it out.

672
01:06:44,802 --> 01:06:49,431
- Henry Jones, Junior.
- Right. That's it, Ox.

673
01:06:49,515 --> 01:06:54,061
Three times <i>down</i>. Downward.

674
01:06:54,436 --> 01:06:56,981
- Three times.
- Pictograms.

675
01:06:58,649 --> 01:07:00,234
Ideograms.

676
01:07:08,784 --> 01:07:10,369
Very good, Ox. Very good.

677
01:07:22,590 --> 01:07:24,258
Ox?

678
01:07:25,426 --> 01:07:27,303
It's me, Ox.

679
01:07:27,386 --> 01:07:32,933
It's Mutt, Ox. Look at me. Look at me. It's me.

680
01:07:34,602 --> 01:07:35,853
It's me.

681
01:07:39,315 --> 01:07:43,235
The wavy lines mean water.
Closed eyes mean sleep.

682
01:07:43,319 --> 01:07:47,615
The sun with an arc across the sky
means time, duration.

683
01:07:47,698 --> 01:07:50,284
The word "now" means "until."

684
01:07:50,367 --> 01:07:51,619
These two together,

685
01:07:51,702 --> 01:07:55,122
the horizon and the snake, form an idea.

686
01:07:55,205 --> 01:07:57,207
The horizon represented the world,

687
01:07:57,291 --> 01:08:00,586
meaning something very large.

688
01:08:02,880 --> 01:08:06,634
"The water sleeps
until the great serpent..."

689
01:08:06,717 --> 01:08:09,762
These aren't just drawings, they're directions.
Give me a map!

690
01:08:14,141 --> 01:08:16,101
The great serpent is
the Amazon, of course,

691
01:08:16,185 --> 01:08:19,229
but what water sleeps?

692
01:08:23,275 --> 01:08:26,695
Here! The Sono!
In Portuguese it means "sleep."

693
01:08:26,779 --> 01:08:28,822
Yes, very good. Exactly.

694
01:08:31,075 --> 01:08:34,912
He wants us to follow
this bend in the Sono...

695
01:08:34,995 --> 01:08:38,290
...to where it meets the Amazon.

696
01:08:38,374 --> 01:08:40,167
After that, I'm not sure.

697
01:08:40,250 --> 01:08:44,672
The kingdom of dreams, tears and eyes.
I don't understand what he means.

698
01:08:44,755 --> 01:08:45,756
This might be the route.

699
01:08:45,839 --> 01:08:48,217
It's completely unexplored territory.

700
01:08:52,596 --> 01:08:54,723
Hurry, hurry! Come on!

701
01:09:01,271 --> 01:09:03,440
- What are we doing?
- They were going to kill us!

702
01:09:03,524 --> 01:09:05,776
- Maybe.
- We had to do something!

703
01:09:05,859 --> 01:09:08,153
- Something <i>else</i> would have been better.
- At least I've got a plan!

704
01:09:08,237 --> 01:09:11,615
This is intolerable.
Harold, don't fall behind!

705
01:09:20,874 --> 01:09:23,711
- Stay back!
- Stop!

706
01:09:23,794 --> 01:09:25,379
Don't move.
When you move you make space and sink.

707
01:09:25,462 --> 01:09:26,714
I think I can get out!

708
01:09:26,797 --> 01:09:28,382
You're pulling against a vacuum.

709
01:09:28,465 --> 01:09:30,592
It's like lifting a car. Calm down.

710
01:09:31,635 --> 01:09:32,928
- Is it quicksand?
- I'm calm.

711
01:09:33,011 --> 01:09:35,013
I'm sinking but I'm calm.

712
01:09:35,097 --> 01:09:37,099
Quicksand is sand,
mud and water.

713
01:09:37,182 --> 01:09:39,059
The viscosity.

714
01:09:39,143 --> 01:09:40,436
For God's sake,

715
01:09:40,519 --> 01:09:42,104
this isn't a classroom!

716
01:09:42,187 --> 01:09:45,149
There's nothing to worry about
unless there's a...

717
01:09:46,984 --> 01:09:48,610
...collapse of the vacuum.

718
01:09:50,154 --> 01:09:52,448
He's going to find something
to pull you out!

719
01:09:52,531 --> 01:09:54,408
Don't just sit there!

720
01:09:54,491 --> 01:09:57,202
For God's sake, get help!

721
01:09:58,078 --> 01:10:00,164
- Help?
- Help!

722
01:10:00,247 --> 01:10:01,749
Help?

723
01:10:01,832 --> 01:10:03,250
Help! Go!

724
01:10:06,295 --> 01:10:08,630
Mutt can be impulsive sometimes.

725
01:10:08,714 --> 01:10:10,841
That's not the worst quality in the world.

726
01:10:11,842 --> 01:10:14,762
Raise your arms.
When the boy gets here, grab hold.

727
01:10:14,845 --> 01:10:17,931
He's a good kid.

728
01:10:18,015 --> 01:10:19,683
Don't insist that he stay in school.

729
01:10:19,767 --> 01:10:21,852
It's not for everyone.

730
01:10:21,935 --> 01:10:23,604
His name is Henry!

731
01:10:23,687 --> 01:10:25,230
That's a good name.

732
01:10:25,314 --> 01:10:26,815
He's your son.

733
01:10:28,859 --> 01:10:30,444
My son?

734
01:10:30,527 --> 01:10:32,404
<i>Henry Jones...</i>

735
01:10:35,574 --> 01:10:39,453
How could you let him
drop out of school?

736
01:10:39,536 --> 01:10:42,206
Mom, grab onto this! Grab her!

737
01:10:42,289 --> 01:10:43,624
I've got her!

738
01:10:44,833 --> 01:10:48,003
Come on, Mom! Pull, pull!

739
01:10:48,086 --> 01:10:49,338
Pull!

740
01:10:52,299 --> 01:10:53,550
Grab it!

741
01:10:55,385 --> 01:10:58,138
- Grab it.
- Grab it, Indy!

742
01:10:58,222 --> 01:10:59,765
It's a rat snake!

743
01:10:59,848 --> 01:11:01,809
They don't get that big!

744
01:11:01,892 --> 01:11:03,477
This one does! Grab it!

745
01:11:03,560 --> 01:11:06,104
- Find something <i>else</i>.
- Like what?

746
01:11:06,188 --> 01:11:07,940
A rope or something.

747
01:11:08,023 --> 01:11:11,235
There's no Sears here!
Grab the snake!

748
01:11:13,612 --> 01:11:16,824
Maybe I can touch bottom.
There's no bottom! Grab it!

749
01:11:16,907 --> 01:11:18,826
No, I can feel it with my feet.

750
01:11:18,909 --> 01:11:21,537
- Grab the snake!
- Stop calling it that!

751
01:11:21,620 --> 01:11:23,330
What am I supposed to call it?

752
01:11:23,413 --> 01:11:25,290
- Call it a "rope."
- What?

753
01:11:25,374 --> 01:11:27,459
Say, "Grab the rope."

754
01:11:27,543 --> 01:11:30,087
Grab the rope!

755
01:11:30,170 --> 01:11:32,089
Hold on tight. It's slippery.

756
01:11:32,965 --> 01:11:34,091
Pull!

757
01:11:40,514 --> 01:11:44,685
Get rid of that thing, will you, son?

758
01:11:44,768 --> 01:11:46,895
You're afraid of snakes.

759
01:11:47,980 --> 01:11:50,023
You're a crazy old man.

760
01:11:53,819 --> 01:11:57,322
Why do you have to complicate
everything, Jonesy?

761
01:12:01,118 --> 01:12:03,161
Well done, Ox. Thank you.

762
01:12:03,871 --> 01:12:04,955
Help.

763
01:12:38,864 --> 01:12:43,076
No. No, he was British.
My dad was an RAF pilot.

764
01:12:43,160 --> 01:12:45,454
He was a hero, not a teacher!

765
01:12:45,537 --> 01:12:49,249
No, sweetheart. Colin was your stepfather.

766
01:12:49,333 --> 01:12:51,126
We started seeing each other
when you were three months old.

767
01:12:51,209 --> 01:12:52,586
He was a good man.

768
01:12:52,669 --> 01:12:56,840
Wait, wait. Colin?
Colin Williams?

769
01:12:58,050 --> 01:13:00,594
You married him? I introduced you!

770
01:13:00,677 --> 01:13:03,764
You gave up your right
to have a say in my husband...

771
01:13:03,847 --> 01:13:06,767
...when you broke up with me
a week before the wedding!

772
01:13:06,850 --> 01:13:09,186
We both knew
it wasn't going to work.

773
01:13:09,269 --> 01:13:11,730
Why did you never tell me about this?

774
01:13:11,813 --> 01:13:13,690
Because I never won an argument!

775
01:13:13,774 --> 01:13:15,525
It's not my fault
you couldn't keep up with me.

776
01:13:15,609 --> 01:13:16,777
I didn't want to hurt you!

777
01:13:16,860 --> 01:13:19,488
For God's sake, just shut up!

778
01:13:19,571 --> 01:13:23,575
Did you never wonder
why Ox stopped talking to you?

779
01:13:23,659 --> 01:13:25,369
He was upset that you ran away.

780
01:13:25,452 --> 01:13:26,870
Stop it, both of you!

781
01:13:26,954 --> 01:13:30,540
The boy shouldn't have to watch
his parents fighting.

782
01:13:30,624 --> 01:13:32,793
- You're not my dad.
- Of course I am.

783
01:13:32,876 --> 01:13:34,211
And I've got news for you:

784
01:13:34,294 --> 01:13:36,296
You're finishing school!

785
01:13:36,380 --> 01:13:37,547
What happened to:

786
01:13:37,631 --> 01:13:39,132
"There's nothing wrong with it...

787
01:13:39,216 --> 01:13:41,051
...and don't let anyone talk you out of it"?

788
01:13:41,927 --> 01:13:43,637
That was before I was your father!

789
01:13:43,720 --> 01:13:45,180
- You're not my father!
- Yes, he is.

790
01:13:45,263 --> 01:13:47,265
You should've told me about him.

791
01:13:48,183 --> 01:13:49,851
You disappeared!

792
01:13:49,935 --> 01:13:52,479
- I wrote to you!
- A year later!

793
01:13:52,562 --> 01:13:56,358
Mutt had already been born
and I was married!

794
01:13:56,441 --> 01:13:58,235
And why are you telling me now?

795
01:13:58,318 --> 01:14:00,654
I thought we were going to die!

796
01:14:00,737 --> 01:14:02,030
Not yet!

797
01:14:16,461 --> 01:14:17,713
Have you got it?

798
01:14:22,092 --> 01:14:23,385
Oh, shit.

799
01:14:35,772 --> 01:14:38,483
I'm sure I wasn't the only one
who went on with their life.

800
01:14:38,567 --> 01:14:41,528
I'm sure there have been
plenty of women in your life.

801
01:14:41,611 --> 01:14:45,824
There were a few.
But they all had the same problem.

802
01:14:45,907 --> 01:14:47,617
What was it?

803
01:14:49,870 --> 01:14:51,663
They weren't you, sweetheart.

804
01:15:16,063 --> 01:15:18,148
We have to rescue Oxley,
get the skull...

805
01:15:18,231 --> 01:15:21,485
...and go to Akator. Drive.

806
01:15:33,163 --> 01:15:35,290
What are you doing?

807
01:15:35,373 --> 01:15:37,667
He never plans ahead.

808
01:15:38,627 --> 01:15:41,755
- Move over, son!
- Don't call me "son."

809
01:15:42,089 --> 01:15:44,049
If I were you, I'd cover my ears.

810
01:15:54,851 --> 01:15:56,269
Duck!

811
01:15:59,856 --> 01:16:01,233
Get out of the way!

812
01:16:03,735 --> 01:16:05,904
Get closer to that amphibious vehicle!

813
01:16:14,830 --> 01:16:17,999
Henry Jones, Junior.

814
01:16:29,886 --> 01:16:31,388
Jones!

815
01:16:46,653 --> 01:16:48,405
Get in!

816
01:17:03,628 --> 01:17:04,963
Duck!

817
01:17:16,808 --> 01:17:19,769
Ox has the skull!
Marion, take the wheel.

818
01:17:19,853 --> 01:17:21,188
But she was driving the truck.

819
01:17:21,271 --> 01:17:24,316
Don't be childish.
Find something to fight with.

820
01:17:34,117 --> 01:17:35,368
Jonesy!

821
01:17:48,715 --> 01:17:50,508
- Jonesy?
- Hello, <i>Mac</i>.

822
01:18:26,044 --> 01:18:29,172
- Indy! Indy!
- Shut up! Shut up!

823
01:18:29,256 --> 01:18:34,302
You stupid son of a bitch! I'm CIA!

824
01:18:34,386 --> 01:18:38,014
I practically shouted it in the tent!
I said, "Just like Berlin."

825
01:18:38,098 --> 01:18:40,058
What were we in Berlin, mate?

826
01:18:40,141 --> 01:18:41,977
- Double agents!
- Throw me the skull!

827
01:18:42,060 --> 01:18:43,812
Throw me the skull!

828
01:19:09,713 --> 01:19:10,964
Riposte.

829
01:19:16,344 --> 01:19:18,471
Your weight
on your front foot. Riposte!

830
01:19:18,555 --> 01:19:20,473
This isn't a competition, Mom.

831
01:19:46,416 --> 01:19:50,587
You fight like all young men,
eager to start and finish!

832
01:19:50,920 --> 01:19:52,547
Mutt, break!

833
01:20:31,961 --> 01:20:34,464
I've got the skull!

834
01:20:34,547 --> 01:20:36,633
Drop the bag!

835
01:20:37,342 --> 01:20:38,802
A gun!

836
01:21:25,348 --> 01:21:28,726
What are you staring at, Daddy?
She's getting away!

837
01:22:27,076 --> 01:22:29,162
This is very dangerous!

838
01:22:57,106 --> 01:22:59,275
<i>Do svidaniya, Dr.</i> Jones.

839
01:23:27,303 --> 01:23:28,805
Jones!

840
01:23:31,391 --> 01:23:32,642
Oh, boy.

841
01:23:33,560 --> 01:23:34,644
Oh, boy.

842
01:23:36,104 --> 01:23:37,188
Ow!

843
01:24:15,476 --> 01:24:16,811
Siafu!

844
01:24:16,894 --> 01:24:18,896
Giant ants! Run!

845
01:24:32,243 --> 01:24:34,037
To the river!

846
01:24:54,223 --> 01:24:55,933
Go to the river!

847
01:25:04,984 --> 01:25:07,195
Get in! Come on!

848
01:25:12,867 --> 01:25:14,661
Mom, there's a cliff!

849
01:27:59,784 --> 01:28:02,328
- Jonesy, hurry!
- Get in, Jones!

850
01:28:18,886 --> 01:28:21,097
- Mom! Stop!
- Slow down!

851
01:28:26,352 --> 01:28:28,020
- Stop! You'll go over!
- That's the idea.

852
01:28:28,104 --> 01:28:30,857
- Bad idea. Give me the wheel.
- Trust me!

853
01:28:59,802 --> 01:29:03,472
- Never do that again.
- Yes, darling.

854
01:29:14,567 --> 01:29:16,819
Three times <i>down</i>.

855
01:29:20,489 --> 01:29:23,492
Reverse! Put it in reverse!
Reverse!

856
01:29:46,223 --> 01:29:47,767
Downward.

857
01:29:49,685 --> 01:29:52,813
It means over land. He came by land.

858
01:29:55,358 --> 01:29:57,610
- Three times down?
- Three times down.

859
01:29:57,693 --> 01:29:59,070
What is he talking about?

860
01:29:59,153 --> 01:30:00,780
He's talking about one!

861
01:30:03,574 --> 01:30:04,867
Two!

862
01:30:23,052 --> 01:30:24,387
Three.

863
01:30:58,254 --> 01:31:01,924
Are you okay? Honey? Let go of that.

864
01:31:02,008 --> 01:31:04,969
"Through eyes that I saw crying."

865
01:31:07,054 --> 01:31:09,265
"The golden vision reappears."

866
01:31:12,935 --> 01:31:14,937
Gold. Count me in.

867
01:31:15,021 --> 01:31:19,650
Through weeping eyes.
We have to go through that waterfall!

868
01:31:21,694 --> 01:31:25,531
We have to return the skull.
I'll go. Nobody else has to.

869
01:31:25,614 --> 01:31:27,783
Who cares?

870
01:31:27,867 --> 01:31:30,745
It's only brought us trouble.

871
01:31:30,828 --> 01:31:33,289
Look what it did to him.

872
01:31:33,372 --> 01:31:36,542
- I have to return it.
- Why you?

873
01:31:36,625 --> 01:31:38,669
Because that's what it told me.

874
01:31:51,474 --> 01:31:55,895
Ochre, iron oxide, charcoal.

875
01:31:55,978 --> 01:31:57,605
How old is this?

876
01:31:57,688 --> 01:32:00,858
Four or five thousand years. Like the pyramids.

877
01:32:01,567 --> 01:32:04,820
Sun worshippers.
Just like the Egyptians.

878
01:32:07,656 --> 01:32:10,868
This is fresh. It was used recently.

879
01:32:24,840 --> 01:32:26,550
More worshippers.

880
01:32:26,634 --> 01:32:29,845
But they're not worshipping the sun.

881
01:32:36,018 --> 01:32:37,394
Someone came...

882
01:32:40,648 --> 01:32:43,818
...and taught the Ugha agriculture.

883
01:32:47,905 --> 01:32:49,406
Irrigation.

884
01:32:59,750 --> 01:33:01,710
Ox, what's wrong?

885
01:33:18,477 --> 01:33:20,604
I think I understand, Ox.

886
01:33:23,149 --> 01:33:24,608
Did someone come?

887
01:33:48,632 --> 01:33:50,134
Are they the same?

888
01:33:50,217 --> 01:33:52,094
There were more. Look.

889
01:33:55,973 --> 01:33:57,766
Thirteen in a circle.

890
01:34:05,107 --> 01:34:06,817
Show us the way, Ox.

891
01:34:55,491 --> 01:34:59,453
- Come on! Come on! Run!
- Come on!

892
01:35:30,025 --> 01:35:31,777
Indy!

893
01:35:31,860 --> 01:35:34,446
Jonesy!

894
01:35:34,530 --> 01:35:35,990
Oxley!

895
01:35:36,991 --> 01:35:40,035
You've been here before.
How did you get out?

896
01:35:51,672 --> 01:35:54,800
Come on. Come on, Mac!

897
01:35:57,553 --> 01:35:58,637
Come on.

898
01:35:58,721 --> 01:36:00,139
"Place your hands upon the key...

899
01:36:00,222 --> 01:36:01,849
...that opens the Palace of Eternity."

900
01:36:01,932 --> 01:36:03,058
Well done, Ox.

901
01:36:27,416 --> 01:36:30,544
The city of gold.
Where's all the gold?

902
01:36:31,879 --> 01:36:33,756
Look at this place.

903
01:36:33,839 --> 01:36:36,633
What a stupid legend.
The time I've wasted.

904
01:36:36,717 --> 01:36:41,513
Ox has been here before
but couldn't get inside the temple.

905
01:36:41,597 --> 01:36:45,434
That's why he returned the skull
to where he found it.

906
01:36:46,226 --> 01:36:49,646
"Place your hands upon the golden key...

907
01:36:49,730 --> 01:36:52,024
...that opens the Palace of Eternity."

908
01:36:52,107 --> 01:36:54,610
The key that opens the palace.

909
01:36:55,944 --> 01:36:58,614
The obelisk. Is the obelisk the key?

910
01:37:06,121 --> 01:37:08,457
What are you looking for, Ox?

911
01:37:10,667 --> 01:37:12,419
What are you doing?

912
01:37:17,299 --> 01:37:20,969
You worked it all out in your cell, didn't you?

913
01:37:21,929 --> 01:37:23,722
Excuse me, Professor.

914
01:37:38,779 --> 01:37:40,114
Well done.

915
01:37:41,323 --> 01:37:43,033
Get your own!

916
01:38:42,217 --> 01:38:43,594
Listen.

917
01:38:45,095 --> 01:38:46,555
Do you hear that?

918
01:38:49,016 --> 01:38:51,185
Get up! Come on! Ox, move!

919
01:38:55,063 --> 01:38:57,816
Hurry! Hurry!

920
01:38:57,900 --> 01:39:00,152
Move it! Move it!

921
01:39:00,235 --> 01:39:01,820
Faster!

922
01:39:01,904 --> 01:39:04,198
This isn't good. Hurry up!

923
01:39:04,823 --> 01:39:06,742
Hurry! Hurry! Hurry!

924
01:39:09,077 --> 01:39:11,705
I don't like this!

925
01:39:11,788 --> 01:39:13,790
Mom, hurry!

926
01:39:31,099 --> 01:39:33,435
He dropped it! He dropped the skull!

927
01:39:45,113 --> 01:39:46,448
Stop this!

928
01:39:49,910 --> 01:39:51,578
Ox, are you all right?

929
01:39:55,374 --> 01:39:56,583
Wonderful.

930
01:39:57,751 --> 01:39:59,628
Well, let's go.

931
01:40:06,635 --> 01:40:07,928
This way?

932
01:41:15,037 --> 01:41:17,039
This is what I was expecting to see.

933
01:41:23,086 --> 01:41:27,007
These are artifacts from every period
of antiquity.

934
01:41:27,090 --> 01:41:29,259
From Macedonia.

935
01:41:29,384 --> 01:41:31,970
- Sumeria.
- This place is a gold mine.

936
01:41:32,054 --> 01:41:34,014
Etruscan. Babylonian.

937
01:41:34,097 --> 01:41:38,226
There isn't a museum
that wouldn't sell its soul for this.

938
01:41:38,310 --> 01:41:39,394
Ancient Egypt.

939
01:41:39,478 --> 01:41:42,439
Dozens of museums.
Hundreds, Jonesy!

940
01:41:43,315 --> 01:41:44,691
Collectors.

941
01:41:47,235 --> 01:41:49,196
They were archaeologists.

942
01:42:26,817 --> 01:42:28,485
How do we open it?

943
01:42:33,949 --> 01:42:37,285
I'll return it to you, Ox. I promise.

944
01:43:56,531 --> 01:43:59,159
The endless waiting is over.

945
01:44:00,535 --> 01:44:02,204
Very soon.

946
01:44:11,963 --> 01:44:13,882
I'm sorry, Jonesy.

947
01:44:13,965 --> 01:44:16,635
What are you, a triple agent?

948
01:44:16,718 --> 01:44:20,055
No, I only lied
when I said I was a double agent.

949
01:44:25,393 --> 01:44:27,187
Look at them!

950
01:44:29,231 --> 01:44:33,902
They're still waiting for the return
of the missing one.

951
01:44:38,240 --> 01:44:40,158
They're a hive mind.

952
01:44:42,118 --> 01:44:46,790
Physically independent beings
with a collective consciousness.

953
01:44:48,583 --> 01:44:51,837
More powerful together
than they are apart.

954
01:45:00,846 --> 01:45:03,431
Imagine what they could tell us.

955
01:45:03,557 --> 01:45:05,267
I can't.

956
01:45:05,350 --> 01:45:06,852
Neither could the people
who built this temple,

957
01:45:06,935 --> 01:45:08,019
and neither can you.

958
01:45:08,103 --> 01:45:11,606
Faith, Dr. Jones,
is a gift you don't possess.

959
01:45:13,149 --> 01:45:14,693
I pity you.

960
01:45:14,776 --> 01:45:18,446
I have faith, sweetheart.
That's why I'm down here.

961
01:45:53,231 --> 01:45:56,484
- He's speaking Mayan.
- What's he saying?

962
01:45:56,568 --> 01:45:59,237
He says he's grateful
and he wants...

963
01:46:01,364 --> 01:46:02,532
"IT"...

964
01:46:05,827 --> 01:46:09,664
That "it" wants to give us a gift.

965
01:46:12,500 --> 01:46:14,336
A great gift.

966
01:46:15,670 --> 01:46:17,797
Tell me everything you know.

967
01:46:21,843 --> 01:46:24,679
I want to know everything. I want to know!

968
01:46:32,854 --> 01:46:35,357
I have a bad feeling about this.

969
01:46:40,028 --> 01:46:41,363
Indy.

970
01:46:42,197 --> 01:46:43,531
The eyes.

971
01:47:48,138 --> 01:47:50,056
What are they, space men?

972
01:47:50,265 --> 01:47:53,685
Interdimensional beings, actually.

973
01:47:53,768 --> 01:47:55,562
Welcome back, Ox!

974
01:48:06,990 --> 01:48:08,658
What the hell is that?

975
01:48:08,742 --> 01:48:12,078
A portal!
A path to another dimension!

976
01:48:12,996 --> 01:48:15,623
I don't think we want to go that way.

977
01:48:31,139 --> 01:48:32,807
I want to know.

978
01:48:33,767 --> 01:48:35,310
I want to know.

979
01:48:36,644 --> 01:48:40,315
Tell me. I'm ready... I want to know.

980
01:48:46,571 --> 01:48:49,908
Mac?

981
01:48:51,743 --> 01:48:52,827
Mac!

982
01:48:55,330 --> 01:48:56,998
Right now!

983
01:49:09,886 --> 01:49:11,346
I can see.

984
01:49:13,223 --> 01:49:14,557
I can see!

985
01:49:17,644 --> 01:49:18,853
Grab him!

986
01:49:23,733 --> 01:49:26,528
Use your legs! I can't do it alone!

987
01:49:28,446 --> 01:49:29,864
Jonesy,

988
01:49:31,324 --> 01:49:33,368
I'm going to get out of this.

989
01:49:42,001 --> 01:49:43,211
No more!

990
01:49:46,714 --> 01:49:48,925
Cover it! Cover it!

991
01:50:02,355 --> 01:50:03,439
Run!

992
01:50:03,523 --> 01:50:05,233
Enough!

993
01:50:05,733 --> 01:50:07,527
Enough!

994
01:50:20,290 --> 01:50:21,791
Run! Run!

995
01:52:42,140 --> 01:52:44,559
Like sweeping away
their footprints.

996
01:52:47,228 --> 01:52:50,606
Where did they go? Into space?

997
01:52:51,441 --> 01:52:56,612
Not into space.
Into the space between spaces.

998
01:53:05,413 --> 01:53:07,206
I don't understand.

999
01:53:08,791 --> 01:53:11,711
What about the legend of a city of gold?

1000
01:53:11,794 --> 01:53:14,964
The word "gold" in Ugha
also means "treasure."

1001
01:53:15,965 --> 01:53:19,135
Their treasure wasn't gold,
it was knowledge.

1002
01:53:19,969 --> 01:53:22,180
Knowledge was their treasure.

1003
01:53:37,904 --> 01:53:40,198
Are we just going to sit here?

1004
01:53:40,281 --> 01:53:41,949
It gets dark fast in the jungle.

1005
01:53:42,033 --> 01:53:44,118
We shouldn't go down in the dark.

1006
01:53:46,537 --> 01:53:50,041
I can.
Who's coming with me? Let's go.

1007
01:53:50,124 --> 01:53:52,627
Why don't you stay, son?

1008
01:53:55,129 --> 01:53:58,091
I don't know.
Why didn't you stay, Dad?

1009
01:54:00,176 --> 01:54:01,636
Dad!

1010
01:54:05,264 --> 01:54:06,599
Dad?

1011
01:54:11,562 --> 01:54:14,148
Somewhere your grandfather is laughing.

1012
01:54:28,454 --> 01:54:30,748
ASSOCIATE DEAN

1013
01:54:30,832 --> 01:54:34,544
Wonderful! Perfect!
Make the letters much bigger.

1014
01:54:47,265 --> 01:54:51,018
Henry Jones, Junior,
and Marion Ravenwood,

1015
01:54:51,102 --> 01:54:56,107
since you have agreed to live together
in holy matrimony...

1016
01:54:56,190 --> 01:54:59,402
...and pledged your love
to each other with these vows,

1017
01:54:59,485 --> 01:55:02,822
joining hands
and exchanging rings,

1018
01:55:02,905 --> 01:55:05,867
I now pronounce you husband and wife.

1019
01:55:06,701 --> 01:55:11,414
What God has joined together,
let no man put asunder.

1020
01:55:11,497 --> 01:55:14,417
"How much of human life
is lost in waiting?"

1021
01:55:15,585 --> 01:55:17,211
Congratulations.

1022
01:55:17,920 --> 01:55:20,047
You may kiss the bride.

1023
01:55:40,610 --> 01:55:42,320
Well done, Henry!

1024
01:55:42,403 --> 01:55:44,405
Thank you, Ox.
